    "q-214044": {
      "dom_id": "step-q214044",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A=\\{1,2,3,4\\}.$ \n\n$$\nR=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4),(3,4)\\}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nIn this relation, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y){{field-1}}R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z){{field-2}}R\\,⟹\\,{{field-3}}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is \n{{field-4}}.\n\nFor example, since $(2,3)$ and $(3,4)$ belong to $R,$ transitivity of $R$ requires \n{{field-5}}\n$\\in R.$",
      "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\n- Consider the pair $(1,2)$ whose second component is $2.$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $2:$ \n\n- Linking $(1,2)$ with $(2,3)$ requires that $(1,3)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Linking $(1,2)$ with $(2,4)$ requires that $(1,4)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(1,3)$ whose second component is $3.$ It can be linked with the following pair from $R$ whose first component is $3:$ \n\n- Linking $(1,3)$ with $(3,4)$ requires that $(1,4)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pair $(1,4)$ whose second component is $4.$ It can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $4.$\n\n- Consider the pair $(2,3)$ whose second component is $3.$ It can be linked with the following pair from $R$ whose first component is $3:$ \n\n- Linking $(2,3)$ with $(3,4)$ requires that $(2,4)\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Consider the pairs $(2,4)$ and $(3,4)$ whose second component is $4.$ They can't be linked with any pair from $R$ since none of them have a first component of $4.$\n\nSo, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is $\\text{transitive}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "select",
          "dom_id": "selectList-214044-0",
          "tag": "custom-select",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "0",
              "dom_id": null,
